.TH TALLY 2
.SH NAME
tally, passed \- count checks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libodin"
.PP
.nf
.B "Tally :: struct { checks: int, failures: int, first_failure: string, later: [8]string, later_count: int }"
.B "tally :: proc(t: ^Tally, ok: bool, what: string) -> bool"
.B "passed :: proc(t: Tally) -> bool"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.B Tally
counts the checks a self-test makes and remembers the first one that
failed.
.I Tally
records one check: it increments
.IR checks ,
and when
.I ok
is
.BR false ,
increments
.I failures
and keeps
.I what
as
.I first_failure
if nothing has failed yet, or in
.I later
otherwise, up to eight more names.
It answers
.I ok
unchanged, so a call site reads
.BR "if !tally(&t, ..., \(dq...\(dq) { return }" ,
which stops the test at the first thing it could not do rather than
reporting confusing failures downstream of it.
.PP
.B The first failure is the one to report.
Later failures are almost always its consequence: a test that opens a
file and then reads it should report that the file did not open, not
that the read came back empty.
A boot log has room for one name per subsystem, and the first is the
right one to keep.
.PP
.I Passed
reports whether a run may print as green:
.I failures
is zero and
.I checks
is greater than zero.
Zero checks is not a pass; a self-test that returned before it tested
anything failed at something the failure counter cannot see, and would
otherwise look identical to a run with nothing to do.
.PP
Every self-test's own result struct embeds a
.B Tally
with
.BR using ,
so
.I r.checks
and
.I r.failures
read as the result's own fields.
.SH EXAMPLES
A kernel self-test's result embeds a
.B Tally
and reports it:
.IP
.EX
Result :: struct {
	using tally: libodin.Tally,
	operations:  int,
}

check :: proc(r: ^Result, ok: bool, what: string) -> bool {
	return libodin.tally(&r.tally, ok, what)
}
.EE
.PP
and the boot log reads it back:
.IP
.EX
if libodin.passed(r.tally) {
	libodin.put_str(&sink, " concurrency checks passed")
} else {
	libodin.put_str(&sink, " FAILED -- first: ")
	libodin.put_str(&sink, r.first_failure)
}
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libodin/tally.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sink (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
None; a
.B Tally
is read directly by its caller rather than through an error value.
